{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And Yosef answered, saying, etc.</b> That is, he answered him regarding what he asserted in the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> ('saying'), for he says he wishes to hear a dream to interpret it — <b>without me</b> (בִּלְעָדַי) things would not be so; but what he said is that Elohim will provide interpretations for dreams through the agency of human beings, as we find that Yosef likewise said to the chief cupbearer and the chief baker (Genesis 40:8): ״הֲלֹא לֵאלֹהִים פִּתְרוֹנִים״ — meaning, the interpretations will not be lacking; and he said ״סַפְּרוּ לִי״ ('tell me'), perhaps an interpretation will be found; and he never singles himself out to say that he is the one who interprets dreams. And likewise he says to him <b>Elohim will answer</b>, etc. And the need to say ״יַעֲנֶה שְׁלוֹם״ ('will answer peace') — he intended to tell him that he should not be distressed if the dream's interpretation foretells evil for him, for from HaShem is the answer of a matter, and it is not dependent on his saying so, for dreams follow the mouth [of the interpreter]. And he gave a reason for saying ״שְׁלוֹם פַּרְעֹה״ ('peace of Pharaoh'), for the king would be changed [adversely affected] by such a matter, and therefore if [the interpretation] foretells evil, the words do not proceed from him. And likewise that is what he says now: <b>Elohim will answer the peace of Pharaoh.</b>" "<b>Further,</b> he further intends in saying <b>Elohim will answer</b>, that the manner of its interpretation is not like the manner of the interpretation of Bar Hedya (Berakhot 56a) and similar cases, where one tilts the dream toward whatever one desires; rather, it is the truth of the meaning of the things. And therefore he said ״אֱלֹהִים״, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:33.807076090Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}